---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---
<Base
  title="mhs — On the fault"
  description="The Coulomb condition a frictional solve enforced at element centroids, read densely across a system of three rough faults, with a mollified kernel and with a classical triangular dislocation element."
  current="onfault"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    <h1>On the fault<span class="sub">the condition, read everywhere</span></h1>
    <p>
      A frictional solve enforces its condition at element centroids and
      nowhere else. This page asks whether the condition holds <i>between</i>
      them: the Coulomb excess <i>&tau;<sub>s</sub></i>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;<i>&mu;&sigma;<sub>n</sub>&prime;</i>,
      which is exactly zero on a slipping centroid by construction, sampled on
      every cell of every facet of a system of three rough faults.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two kernels, one solve, one window. The mollified kernel is bounded on the
      whole surface. The classical element is finite at its own centroid &mdash;
      and that is the only place it is.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      Colour is <b>data</b>: one value per cell, nothing interpolated, no light.
      The <b>mask</b> blanks every cell closer than <i>d</i>&nbsp;eps to a facet
      edge, which is where a piecewise-constant slip leaves its stripes on
      <i>both</i> kernels, so the facet cores can be read on their own.
    </div>
    <p class="contact">
      The legend states the saturated area at the current window for both
      kernels at once; move the window and watch which one moves.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <div id="viewer" class="onfault">
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="kernel"><span>kernel</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="metric"><span>metric</span></label>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="window"><span class="lbl">window</span></div>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="mask"><span class="lbl">mask</span></div>
    </div>
    <div data-canvas class="canvas" aria-label="Three rough faults coloured by on-fault Coulomb excess, one value per cell, for the mollified and the classical kernel"></div>
    <p data-legend class="legend"></p>
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="mark"><span>mark saturated</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="wire"><span>facet edges</span></label>
      <span class="lbl">view</span>
      <span class="btns" data-ctl="view"></span>
      <span class="btns" data-ctl="shot"></span>
      <label data-ctl="publication"><span>publication view</span></label>
    </div>
    <p data-status class="status">loading&hellip;</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What is being read, and why it is the sharpest test</h2>
  <p>
    Every other page on this site evaluates stress <i>off</i> the fault. The
    result that motivates a mollified kernel in the first place is <i>on</i> it.
    A constant-slip triangular element is finite at its own centroid for a
    geometric reason: the centroid sits a fixed 0.29&nbsp;<i>h</i> from the
    element&rsquo;s own edges, and so a fixed distance from the 1/<i>d</i>
    singularity of its bounding dislocation loop. That is why a classical
    matrix collocated at centroids is a perfectly usable one, and why the two
    kernels&rsquo; solutions here agree at the centroids to about a per cent.
    But finite at one point per element is not <i>well behaved</i>: everywhere
    else on the surface the classical field is the field of the residual
    dislocations on the shared edges, and it diverges there. The mollified
    field is bounded on the whole surface, with <i>eps</i> a resolution floor
    that does not depend on <i>h</i> &mdash; so the condition the solve
    enforced can be <b>checked</b> at every point of the fault. No readout in
    either repository had done that before this page.
  </p>
  <p>
    The condition is Coulomb. At the load &lambda; the solve finds the slip
    &delta; on every element such that each is either locked and below
    strength, or slipping and exactly at it:
    <i>w</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<i>&mu;</i>(<i>&sigma;</i><sub>eff,0</sub>&nbsp;+&nbsp;&lambda;<i>&sigma;<sub>n</sub></i><sup>load</sup>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;<i>N</i><sup>T</sup>&delta;)&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;(&lambda;|<i>&tau;</i><sup>load</sup>|&nbsp;+&nbsp;<i>P</i><sup>T</sup>&delta;)&nbsp;&ge;&nbsp;0,
    with &delta;&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;<i>w</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0. The slip-induced
    normal stress <i>N</i><sup>T</sup>&delta; is <b>coupled in</b>, so the
    strength an element must reach depends on how its neighbours have clamped
    or unclamped it; that keeps the problem a linear complementarity problem,
    and its uniqueness needs sym(&minus;<i>P</i><sub>eff</sub>) positive
    definite, which it is here for both kernels (smallest eigenvalue +5.16
    mollified, +5.21 classical). The quantity on the canvas is
    <i>S</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<i>&tau;<sub>s</sub></i>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;<i>&mu;&sigma;<sub>n</sub>&prime;</i>,
    assembled from the remote load and the slip-induced traction at each cell,
    with the same signs and the same friction as the solver &mdash; so that at
    a centroid it is <b>exactly &minus;<i>w</i></b>, zero to the solver&rsquo;s
    10<sup>&minus;9</sup>&nbsp;GPa on every slipping element, negative on every
    locked one. Between the centroids it is the solution&rsquo;s own residual.
  </p>

  <h2>The system</h2>
  <p>
    Three rough discs, each with the published roughness (rms facet slope 0.25,
    correlation length 8&nbsp;<i>h</i>, facet tilt rms 13.9&deg;), at
    <i>h</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.5&nbsp;km and <i>eps</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.05&nbsp;km,
    under the published thrust load with friction 0.6 and an effective normal
    stress of 150&nbsp;MPa:
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>fault</th><th>radius</th><th>dip</th><th>elements</th><th>role</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>A</b></td><td>6 km</td><td>30&deg;</td><td>1044</td>
            <td>the published crack of the roughness and isosurface pages, unchanged</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>B</b></td><td>4 km</td><td>30&deg;</td><td>450</td>
            <td>a parallel ramp 2 km into A&rsquo;s hanging wall, where A&rsquo;s slip shadows it</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>C</b></td><td>3 km</td><td>50&deg;</td><td>258</td>
            <td>a steeper splay above A&rsquo;s up-dip edge, misoriented for this load</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    1752 elements; the closest any vertex of one fault comes to a facet of
    another is 1.04&nbsp;km, 20&nbsp;<i>eps</i>, so no fault sits inside
    another&rsquo;s mollification band and every interaction is elastic. The
    load is set by a rule, not by eye: the smallest multiple of the
    system&rsquo;s first failure at which A slips over most of its area and B
    and C are each partly locked and partly slipping. That is
    &lambda;&nbsp;=&nbsp;1.20&nbsp;&lambda;<sub>0</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;141&nbsp;MPa
    of tectonic shear; at 1.05 the splay does not slip at all, and at 1.35 B is
    fully active. Solving each fault <i>alone</i> on the same matrix says what
    the others do to it:
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>fault</th><th>active alone</th><th>active in the system</th>
            <th>moment</th><th>Coulomb load from the others</th><th>max slip</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>A</b></td><td>0.96</td><td>0.95</td><td>&times;0.97</td>
            <td>&minus;0.23 MPa</td><td>2.62 m</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>B</b></td><td>0.92</td><td>0.83</td><td><b>&times;0.79</b></td>
            <td><b>&minus;2.13 MPa</b></td><td>1.22 m</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>C</b></td><td>0.07</td><td>0.08</td><td>&times;1.12</td>
            <td><b>+0.71 MPa</b></td><td>0.19 m</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    B loses a fifth of its moment to A&rsquo;s shadow. C is loaded by the
    others and slips a little more than it would alone &mdash; a misoriented
    splay that moves only where the system pushes it, which is why it is here.
    The classical kernel gives the same table to the second decimal: at the
    centroids, where it is finite, it is a fine kernel. Switch the metric to
    the normal-stress change to see why the coupling is in the solve: the
    slip on A clamps and unclamps its own rough surface by
    <b>&minus;22 to +23&nbsp;MPa</b>, three times the Coulomb excess it is
    relieving, and B by &minus;16 to +18.
  </p>

  <h2>What the maps show</h2>
  <p>
    The page opens on the Coulomb excess at a window of &plusmn;24&nbsp;MPa, the
    99th percentile of the mollified map over the slipping facets. For scale:
    the remote load resolves 116&nbsp;MPa of shear onto fault A against a
    strength of 108, so the slip is relieving an excess of about
    <b>7&nbsp;MPa</b> &mdash; and in doing so it changes the shear on the
    rough surface by tens of MPa in both senses, which is why the residual
    between the centroids is not small compared with the excess. On the
    slipping facets, 79&nbsp;% of the area, that residual is:
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>from the nearest facet edge</th><th>share of slipping area</th>
            <th>mollified |<i>S</i>|, median</th><th>classical |<i>S</i>|, median</th>
            <th>|classical &minus; mollified|, median</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>&lt; 0.5 <i>eps</i></td><td>27 %</td><td>5.0 MPa</td><td><b>33.7 MPa</b></td><td>29.7 MPa</td></tr>
        <tr><td>0.5&ndash;1 <i>eps</i></td><td>24 %</td><td>6.2</td><td>16.7</td><td>10.7</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1&ndash;1.5 <i>eps</i></td><td>32 %</td><td>4.8</td><td>6.6</td><td>1.8</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.5&ndash;2 <i>eps</i></td><td>1 %</td><td>4.8</td><td>5.9</td><td>1.1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&ge; 2 <i>eps</i>, the cores</td><td>16 %</td><td><b>1.9</b></td><td><b>2.3</b></td><td><b>0.35</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>all</td><td>100 %</td><td>4.4 &middot; p99 24 &middot; max 52</td>
            <td>9.9 &middot; p99 113 &middot; max 350</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Read the two columns against distance. The mollified one is <b>flat</b>:
    five or six MPa within an <i>eps</i> of an edge, two in the cores, nothing
    larger than 52 anywhere &mdash; bounded, as the kernel is. The classical
    one rises <b>fifteenfold</b> from the cores to the nearest cells, which is
    what 1/<i>d</i> sampled at 0.29&nbsp;<i>eps</i> looks like, and would rise
    further on a finer lattice. In the cores, where neither kernel is near an
    edge, the two agree to 0.35&nbsp;MPa on a 2&nbsp;MPa residual: the same
    staircase, integrated by two kernels that agree where the geometry lets
    them. At the opening window the classical map saturates <b>26&nbsp;%</b> of
    the slipping area and the mollified map <b>0.9&nbsp;%</b>; move the mask
    to one <i>eps</i> and the classical figure falls to 1.8&nbsp;%, because
    every cell it was saturating was within an <i>eps</i> of an edge. The
    locked facets saturate blue under both kernels &mdash; a median of 21&nbsp;MPa
    below strength, which is the correct reading of them &mdash; and they are
    the rest of the 10&nbsp;% and 30&nbsp;% the legend reports.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more thing the maps show that a table of centroids cannot: a facet
    the solve holds <i>locked</i> can read above strength between its
    centroids &mdash; by up to 11&nbsp;MPa under the mollified kernel and
    59&nbsp;MPa under the classical one. That is the staircase again, and it
    is where a finer density or a smaller element would go to work.
  </p>

  <h2>Both kernels are staircases; only one is bounded</h2>
  <p>
    A piecewise-constant slip is a staircase, and two neighbouring facets that
    slip by different amounts leave a residual dislocation on the edge they
    share &mdash; the vector difference of the two slips, which on a rough mesh
    includes the rotation of the slip direction across the kink. So <b>both</b>
    kernels carry a stripe at every interior edge with a slip mismatch, and the
    page does not pretend otherwise. What differs is what the stripe is. For
    the mollified kernel it is a bounded bump of order
    <i>&mu;</i>&Delta;<i>b</i>/<i>eps</i>: measured at the worst edge of the
    published crack, 6&nbsp;MPa, against the classical 3.95&nbsp;GPa at 5&nbsp;cm
    from the same edge. Measured on this system, cell by cell: within an
    <i>eps</i> of an edge the mollified residual is two and a half times its
    value in the facet cores and never exceeds 52&nbsp;MPa; the classical one is
    fifteen times its core value at the nearest cells and reaches 350.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <b>mask</b> is how the reader separates the two statements. Blank every
    cell within one <i>eps</i> of a facet edge and what remains is the facet
    cores &mdash; where a bounded kernel and an unbounded one must agree if
    they are integrating the same slip. They do, to 0.35&nbsp;MPa, and at a
    mask of 1.5&nbsp;<i>eps</i> the two maps are the same map. A P1 density
    would shrink the mollified staircase; the classical one is unbounded at
    any order.
  </p>

  <h2>Calibration on the one crack with a closed form</h2>
  <p>
    A frictionless penny crack under a uniform stress drop has an exact
    answer on the fault: the induced shear along the slip direction is
    &minus;&Delta;<i>&tau;</i> everywhere inside. So the selfcheck solves
    one &mdash; radius 1&nbsp;km, <i>h</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<i>a</i>/8,
    <i>eps</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.1&nbsp;<i>h</i>, dipping 30&deg; so that neither
    kernel sits in its degenerate frame &mdash; with each kernel, reads both
    densely, and bins the departure from that answer by distance to the
    nearest facet edge and to the rim. Medians of
    |residual|/&Delta;<i>&tau;</i>:
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>kernel</th><th>from the nearest edge</th>
            <th>&lt; 2 <i>eps</i> from the rim</th><th>2&ndash;10 <i>eps</i></th>
            <th>&ge; 10 <i>eps</i></th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td rowspan="3">mollified</td><td>&lt; 1 <i>eps</i></td>
            <td>1.70</td><td>1.06</td><td>0.35</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1&ndash;2 <i>eps</i></td><td>1.66</td><td>0.98</td><td>0.30</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&ge; 2 <i>eps</i> (the cores)</td><td>0.57</td><td>0.28</td><td><b>0.084</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td rowspan="3">classical</td><td>&lt; 1 <i>eps</i></td>
            <td>7.48</td><td>3.15</td><td>0.96</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1&ndash;2 <i>eps</i></td><td>2.96</td><td>1.48</td><td>0.44</td></tr>
        <tr><td>&ge; 2 <i>eps</i> (the cores)</td><td>0.67</td><td>0.34</td><td><b>0.097</b></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Three things are in that table. In the facet cores, away from the rim,
    <b>both kernels see the same staircase</b> &mdash; 8.4&nbsp;% and
    9.7&nbsp;% of the drop &mdash; and that number is the discretisation&rsquo;s,
    not either kernel&rsquo;s: impose a <i>uniform</i> slip, which leaves no
    residual on any interior edge, and the two kernels agree to <b>1.2&nbsp;%</b>
    at every distance from an edge, the nearest cells included. Within an
    <i>eps</i> of an edge the mollified residual is 35&nbsp;% of the drop and
    bounded; the classical one is 96&nbsp;% there and still rising as
    1/<i>d</i>, the nearest cell on this mesh being 0.58&nbsp;<i>eps</i> out.
    And at the rim, which <i>is</i> a dislocation line and a physical one, the
    mollified residual is 1.7 times the drop against 7.5 for the classical
    kernel. Each of those is a gate in the selfcheck, pinned at 1.5 times the
    value measured.
  </p>

  <h2>Signs, windows, and what is not shown</h2>
  <p>
    Traction is <i>&sigma;</i>&middot;<i>n</i> with each facet&rsquo;s upward
    normal, so its normal component is tension positive; the normal stress in
    the Coulomb strength is compression positive, which is the sign the
    friction module uses, and the two are pinned against each other rather
    than reasoned about. The window is symmetric, &plusmn;<i>W</i>, with
    <i>W</i> the area-weighted 99th percentile of the <i>mollified</i> map
    &mdash; over slipping facets for the Coulomb excess, so that locked facets
    saturate blue, which is the correct reading of them &mdash; and it is the
    same window for both kernels. The slider moves it; the legend counts what
    saturates.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing here is smoothed, decimated or interpolated: the cells are
    coloured with the sample taken at their centroid, the nearest of which sits
    0.29&nbsp;<i>eps</i> from a facet edge. The classical values are
    therefore as large as that spacing lets them be, exactly as the grid
    spacing set them on the isosurface page, and the stated cell size is the
    one number a reader needs to scale them.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    Every array ships with its byte count and sha256 in
    <a href={`${base}data/onfault_manifest.json`}>onfault_manifest.json</a>,
    with the load, the ladder it was chosen from, the window rule and both
    kernels&rsquo; definiteness, sweep counts and residuals. See
    <a href={`${base}roughness/`}>Rough crack</a> for the kernel head-to-head at
    the centroids, <a href={`${base}isosurface/`}>Isosurfaces</a> for the same
    disagreement off the fault, and <a href={`${base}concepts/`}>Concepts</a> for
    what <i>eps</i> is.
  </p>

  <script>
    import { startOnFault } from "../viz/onfault";
    startOnFault(document.getElementById("viewer")!, import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
  </script>
</Base>
